<!--
    RE-friction round 2, need `simd-constant-string-initializer`, challenge
    653d88600f4238b24302b0ec (macOS x86-64 Crackme0x04), sub_100003790 at
    0x100003790  -  the 752-byte initializer that builds two consecutive
    character ranges and then spells the crackme's prompt and result messages
    out of them.  Verbatim bytes; the second chunk is the SSE constant table
    at 0x100003f30 the vectorised body indexes ({0,1,...,15}, {16 x 16},
    {32 x 16}).

    Option `simdlane` (P3 simplification-quiescence).  The compiler broadcasts
    one byte across an XMM register with the standard idiom

        66 0f ef d2        pxor   xmm2,xmm2
        66 0f 38 00 c2     pshufb xmm0,xmm2

    and `pshufb` has no p-code semantics: the x86 SLEIGH spec models it as an
    opaque CALLOTHER user op over a 16-byte value.  ActionLaneDivide then splits
    the vector consumers into byte lanes, so every lane of the result becomes
    its own SUBPIECE of something nothing downstream can see through, and the
    body comes back as thirty-two `SUB161(v7,k)` temporaries  -  thirty of them
    declared only to be read once  -  that all name the SAME byte.

    The instruction is a pure permutation with an exact per-lane definition once
    the mask is known, so a one-byte lane read of a constant-mask shuffle IS a
    lane read of its source.  With the option on all thirty-two resolve to
    `dat_100008040` / `dat_100008041` and the lane temporaries and their
    declarations are gone.

    Two passes over one function: pass 1 pins the upstream rendering with
    `option simdlane off`, pass 2 the shipped default.  Counts are over the
    whole transcript, so a shape present in only one pass pins that pass.
-->
